        "name": "truedone",
        "description": "truedone checks whether a pull request's tests prove its change: red without the change, green with it. It also flags tests that were skipped, focused or weakened. Each pull request gets one verdict, as a check run and a label, so you see what the tests show before you read the diff.\r\n\r\n**On each pull request**\r\n\r\n- A check run named truedone, with the verdict and the reason for it.\r\n- One label: `truedone:yes`, `truedone:weak`, `truedone:tampered`, `truedone:review`, `truedone:no-evidence`, `truedone:unrun`, `truedone:pending` or `truedone:could-not-run`. A pull request with nothing to check gets none.\r\n\r\n**Why each permission**\r\n\r\n- Pull requests, write: read the changed files and set the label.\r\n- Checks, write: post the check run.\r\n- Contents, read: read each changed test at the base and at the head.\r\n- Actions, read: read the report the truedone action uploads, for the red-green verdict.\r\n- Metadata, read: GitHub requires it.\r\n\r\nIt never runs your code. The static checks only read files. The red-green run happens in your own workflow, through the truedone action.\r\n\r\n**To turn it off** for one repository, add `enabled: false` to `.github/truedone.yml` on your default branch, or uninstall the app.\r\n\r\nThis is a preview. [truedone.pages.dev](https://truedone.pages.dev) shows what works today. Source: [github.com/Truedone/truedone](https://github.com/Truedone/truedone) (Apache-2.0). [Privacy](https://truedone.pages.dev/privacy) and [terms](https://truedone.pages.dev/terms).",
